Optional backend optimisation for the cloud fog of war — NOT YET DEPLOYED.

Context
-------
The fog is now drawn as the whole screen minus holes for explored ground, so
unexplored region geometry is never rendered. The client already discards it on
arrival and already sends the `ids` it actually wants, but the deployed function
destructures only the bounds, so today the full result set is still transferred
and JSON-parsed on the main isolate before being thrown away.

Applying this patch makes `/regions/viewport` honour that filter. It is purely
an optimisation: the client works correctly against both versions, so this can
be deployed whenever the team is ready and needs no client release.

`ids` is optional — omitting it preserves the current behaviour exactly, so any
other caller is unaffected.

Apply with:  git apply functions/viewport-id-filter.patch
Deploy with: firebase deploy --only functions

--- a/functions/index.js
+++ b/functions/index.js
@@ -125,7 +125,7 @@
 app.post('/regions/viewport', async (req, res) => {
   try {
-    const { south, west, north, east } = req.body;
+    const { south, west, north, east, ids } = req.body;

     if ([south, west, north, east].some((value) => value == null)) {
       return res.status(400).json({
         error: 'south, west, north, and east are required',
       });
     }

+    // Callers that only render explored regions pass the ids they want. Without
+    // this the whole viewport is serialised and discarded client-side.
+    const filterIds = Array.isArray(ids) && ids.length > 0 ? ids : null;
+
     const query = `
       SELECT
         id,
         name,
         ST_Area(geography(geometry)) AS area_square_metres,
         ST_AsGeoJSON(geometry) AS geometry
       FROM regions
       WHERE ST_Intersects(
         geometry,
         ST_MakeEnvelope($1, $2, $3, $4, 4326)
       )
+      ${filterIds ? 'AND id = ANY($5)' : ''}
       LIMIT 650;
     `;

-    const result = await getPool().query(query, [west, south, east, north]);
+    const params = [west, south, east, north];
+    if (filterIds) params.push(filterIds);
+
+    const result = await getPool().query(query, params);
     return res.json(result.rows);
   } catch (error) {
     console.error('Error in /regions/viewport:', error);

Note on the local dev twin
--------------------------
spatial-api/index.js has the same handler with LIMIT 80. Apply the equivalent
change there too, or local and deployed behaviour will keep diverging.

Note on the area contract test
------------------------------
test/backend/region_area_query_test.dart reads both files as text and asserts
they contain `ST_Area(geography(geometry)) AS area_square_metres`. This patch
leaves that expression untouched, so the test keeps passing.
